<Layout title="How we model SPDTF" description="The evidence-up semantic-modelling method: authority, competency questions, candidate construction, human review, traceability and versioning.">
  <PageMeta category="How we model SPDTF" variant="brand" updated="Method documentation · authority varies by decision record" />
  <h1>How we model SPDTF</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    SPDTF starts with practitioner questions and attributed evidence, not with a
    preselected JSON tree. Facilitators turn that evidence into a reviewable semantic
    package; automated tools encode and check it; people decide whether the meaning fits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="authority">Authority of this method</h2>
  <p>
    The method is assembled from decisions with different standing. This page labels the
    boundary so an intended workflow is not mistaken for an adopted lifecycle.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Decision status behind the method</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Record</th><th scope="col">Standing</th><th scope="col">What may be stated now</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0063">ADR-0063</a></th><td>Accepted</td><td>Domain-led bounded contexts, six semantic outputs, eight concerns, four dispositions and the evidence-led working sequence.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0067">ADR-0067</a></th><td>Accepted for the Property Pack</td><td>First-principles Property Pack candidate architecture, context ownership, technical profile and deterministic checks.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0065">ADR-0065</a></th><td>Proposed</td><td>Human-led, AI-assisted workflow is a proposed SPDTF-wide operating model, not current policy.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="/modelling/adr/adr-0068">ADR-0068</a></th><td>Proposed</td><td>Candidate lifecycle and promotion stages remain proposed; no page may imply they are operative.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout callout--key">
    <p class="callout__label">Current ceiling</p>
    <p>Outside the separately governed Property Pack determination, the safe lifecycle ceiling is a first working-group draft—non-normative—until accepted governance defines later promotion.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="questions">Competency questions before structures</h2>
  <p>
    A competency question is a business question the model should be able to answer. It
    prevents modelling for its own sake and provides a testable reason for each important
    distinction. Questions are written in practitioner language before terms are turned
    into classes or properties.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Which property does this legal title apply to, and what source supports that link?</li>
    <li>Which organisation supplied this fact, when was it recorded, and for what purpose?</li>
    <li>Which meanings are local to conveyancing and which must cross into lending?</li>
    <li>Which controlled value set governs the status, and which version was used?</li>
    <li>Which rule is a business expectation for a delivery rather than a universal truth?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each governed question must record a context owner, evidence, expected answer pattern and review state.
    A query can later test whether the candidate structure supports the question, but a
    successful query does not prove that the proposed answer is correct business policy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cycle">Evidence-up modelling cycle</h2>
  <Diagram size="large" caption="The accepted working sequence begins with scoped questions and attributed evidence. A candidate passes technical checks before human review, but only a recorded disposition changes its standing. Review may return the work for more evidence.">
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Evidence-up modelling cycle for SPDTF
  accDescr: Scoped questions and attributed evidence lead to a semantic candidate, automated checks, human review and a recorded disposition, with feedback returning to the evidence stage.
  Questions["Scope and competency questions"]:::user
  Evidence["Attributed evidence"]:::external
  Candidate["Semantic package candidate"]:::process
  Checks["Declared automated checks"]:::data
  Review["Practitioner review"]:::user
  Disposition["Recorded disposition and version"]:::security
  Questions --> Evidence
  Evidence --> Candidate
  Candidate --> Checks
  Checks --> Review
  Review --> Disposition
  Review -.->|needs evidence or revision| Evidence
  </Diagram>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Scope:</strong> name the context, owner, business questions and out-of-scope concerns.</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> collect participant language, examples, counterexamples, documents, datasets and prior PDTF material with provenance.</li>
    <li><strong>Candidate:</strong> align glossary, dictionary, taxonomies, vocabularies, resources and relationships.</li>
    <li><strong>Encode:</strong> represent the candidate with the declared RDF/OWL, SKOS and SHACL feature boundary.</li>
    <li><strong>Check:</strong> run parsing, constraint, coverage and competency-query tests with reproducible receipts.</li>
    <li><strong>Review:</strong> publish readable definitions, diagrams, evidence and open questions to the relevant practitioners.</li>
    <li><strong>Record a review outcome:</strong> acceptable for this draft, needs evidence, revise, defer or not accepted; preserve the immutable diff. These review outcomes are not the four formal coverage dispositions.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence hierarchy and uncertainty</h2>
  <p>
    Evidence may come from participant sessions, operational documents, statutory or
    authoritative sources, data dictionaries, examples, the PDTF schema corpus, external
    standards or machine extraction. Its role must be recorded. Prior implementation is
    useful evidence, but it cannot silently decide new meaning.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Evidence handling record</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Purpose</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Source and version</th><td>Identifies exactly what was examined and when.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Source type</th><td>Distinguishes participant evidence, authority, implementation, research and machine-derived material.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Permission and sensitivity</th><td>Controls whether and how evidence can be retained or published.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Claim and confidence</th><td>Separates what the source says from the facilitator’s interpretation.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Counterexample or dissent</th><td>Preserves disagreement instead of averaging it away.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Candidate impact</th><td>Links evidence to the exact term, relationship, constraint or open question.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="untrusted-evidence">Untrusted evidence and isolated work orders</h2>
  <p>
    ADR-0067 treats every uploaded file, email, transcript, standard, spreadsheet,
    schema and legacy artefact as untrusted data. Supplier identity does not turn
    document text into an instruction. A bounded work order keeps evidence separate
    from controls and lets people review what entered the candidate.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Record source identity, media type, content digest, cited source span, permission, sensitivity and resource limits.</li>
    <li>Delimit evidence as data; quarantine secrets, prohibited personal data, instruction-like content, unsafe archives and unresolved licensing.</li>
    <li>Carry provenance and taint labels into every derivative instead of silently obeying or discarding a finding.</li>
    <li>Bind each work order to allowed read and write graphs, retained concerns, questions, route, budget, expected outputs, checks and a human decision owner.</li>
    <li>Write only an isolated candidate delta. Tools and agents cannot edit an accepted corpus, common boundary, evaluator, policy or release.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="responsibilities">Human and machine responsibilities</h2>
  <table>
    <caption>Assistance does not transfer authority</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Tools may</th><th scope="col">People must</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Extraction</th><td>Find candidate terms, fields, examples and apparent relationships.</td><td>Confirm relevance, source interpretation and missing context.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Comparison</th><td>Detect duplicates, name collisions and possible external matches.</td><td>Decide identity, distinction, ownership and mapping strength.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Encoding</th><td>Generate deterministic RDF, SKOS, SHACL, queries and readable views.</td><td>Approve the bounded feature profile and review the result.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Validation</th><td>Run declared checks and produce reproducible reports.</td><td>Judge meaning, risk, exceptions and governance disposition.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    For cross-context comparisons, use the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/evidence-and-mappings#cross-context-mappings">Category 8 mapping workflow</a>:
    tools may suggest candidates, but people choose the predicate and approve the evidence record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="receipt">Candidate receipt and immutable cut</h2>
  <p>
    A reviewable cut needs a version identifier, semantic owner, source manifest,
    competency questions, standards-profile version, six-output manifest, coverage
    receipt, validation report, open questions, review record and change diff. Derived
    web pages and schemas cite that package; they do not become new sources of meaning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Continue with the <a href="/semantic-modelling/semantic-package">six-part semantic package</a>
    or the detailed <a href="/semantic-modelling/modelling-rules">modelling rules and lenses</a>.
  </p>
